                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
                has to have vanishing square. By linearity this implies that
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mn 0
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi Ψ
                                %mn 1
                              %mo +
                              %msub
                                %mi Ψ
                                %mn 2
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mn 2
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %munder
                                %munder
                                  %mrow
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %msub
                                      %mi Ψ
                                      %mn 1
                                    %msup
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                      %mn 2
                                  %mo ⏟
                                %mrow
                                  %mo =
                                  %mn 0
                              %mo +
                              %munder
                                %munder
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi Ψ
                                      %mn 1
                                    %msub
                                      %mi Ψ
                                      %mn 2
                                    %mo +
                                    %msub
                                      %mi Ψ
                                      %mn 2
                                    %msub
                                      %mi Ψ
                                      %mn 1
                                  %mo ⏟
                                %mrow
                                  %mo =
                                  %mn 0
                              %mo +
                              %munder
                                %munder
                                  %mrow
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %msub
                                      %mi Ψ
                                      %mn 2
                                    %msup
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                      %mn 2
                                  %mo ⏟
                                %mrow
                                  %mo =
                                  %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\begin{aligned}
                      0 &amp; = (\Psi_1 + \Psi_2)^2
                      \\\
                      \&amp; =
                      \\underset{ = 0}{\underbrace{ (\Psi_1)^2 }}
                      +
                      \\underset{ = 0}{\underbrace{ \Psi_1 \Psi_2 + \Psi_2 \Psi_1 }}
                      +
                      \\underset{ = 0}{\underbrace{ (\Psi_2)^2 }}
                      \\end{aligned}
              %p
                and hence that fermionic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+functions"} wave functions
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+commutative+algebra"} anti-commute
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+superalgebra"} commutative superalgebra
                (a
                %em slightly
                = succeed "!)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebra"} noncommutative algebra
              %p
                Ever since the existence of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermionic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
                was
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experimentally
                established, around the time of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stern-Gerlach+experiment"} Stern-Gerlach experiment
                in the 1920s, it is thus an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experimental
                fact that fundamental physics is described by
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
                %em not
                necessarily
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} super
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} symmetric
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics+--+supersymmetry#SupersymmetryFromTheSuperpoint"} close relation
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/Higher+Structures"} Higher structures
                is short for
                %em
                  higher
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theoretic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structures"} structures
                and reflects the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+principle"} gauge principle
                of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                (e.g.
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+histories"} field histories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equal
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge transformation
                between them, mathematically a
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi γ
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      x \stackrel{\gamma}{\longrightarrow} y
              %p
                This principle applies also to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
                themselves, and thus leads to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge-of-gauge+transformations"} gauge-of-gauge transformations
              %center
                %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/2Cell.jpg", :width => "200"}/
              %p
                and so on to ever
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+transformations"} higher gauge transformations
              %center
                %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/3Cell.jpg", :width => "200"}/
              %p
                mathematically reflected by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+homotopies"} higher homotopies
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} higher homotopy types
              %p
                Ever since the existence of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge fields
                was understood in the 1920s, it is thus an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experimental
                fact that fundamental physics is described by
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
          %p
            A striking consequence is that, both in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            as well as in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            and therefore in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"} geometry of fundamental physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            generally are
            %em not
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/points"} points
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+manifolds"} differentiable manifolds
          %p
            What, then,
            %em is
            the
            = succeed "?" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"} geometry of fundamental physics
          %p
            The right framework to answer questions like this has been
            %em urged
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Alexander Grothendieck
            already in
            %a{:href => "functorial+geometry#Grothendieck73"} Grothendieck 73
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "functorial+geometry#Lawvere16Quote"} Lawvere 03
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
            (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toposes+of+laws+of+motion"} Lawvere 97
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Some+Thoughts+on+the+Future+of+Category+Theory"} Lawvere 91
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Structured+Spaces"}> Lurie 09
            ,
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+a+cohesive+topos"} S. 13
          %p
            The answer is known, alternatively, as (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"}> higher
            )
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+geometry"} functorial geometry
            (Grothendieck) or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gros+toposes"} gros toposes
            (Lawvere), or variants thereof.
          %p
            In this lecture series we try to give a self-contained introduction to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            this way, following
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+a+cohesive+topos"} Schreiber 13
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            and will provide brief indication of real-world applications to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+super+p-branes"} fundamental super p-branes
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            which we lay out subsumes various special cases that are discussed separately in the literature:
