          %h2#AboutThisPage About this text
          %p
            This page is going to contain an introduction to aspects of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            and their application in fundamental
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            appearing in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+geometry"} Riemannian geometry
            appearing in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            appearing in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of both.
          %h3#scope_and_perspective Scope and perspective
          %p
            The intended topic scope and readership of the first layer of this page – the
            %em
              %a{:href => "#LayerMod"} Model Layer
            – is much like that of the book (
            %a{:href => "#Frankel"}> Frankel
            ), only that here we make use of a more modern and more transparent conceptual toolbox. We also discuss in two other layers, the
            %em
              %a{:href => "#LayerSem"} Semantic Layer
            and the
            %em
              %a{:href => "#LayerSyn"} Syntactic Layer
            deeper mechanisms at work in the background.
          %p
            Notably, where traditional expositions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            proceed by generalizing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            of abstract
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate"} coordinate systems
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "SmoothSpacesLayerMod"} Smooth sets – Model Layer
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
            %em
              %a{:href => "#SmoothSpacesLayerSem"} Smooth sets – Semantic Layer
            how this means that we are working in the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
            over abstract coordinate systems. Smooth manifolds are then introduced later as an intermediate notion, together with that of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            applied in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            do not actually need the notion of a smooth manifold, and, more importantly, for many notions in modern theoretical physics smooth manifolds are not actually sufficiently general.)
          %p
            In fact we introduce smooth manifolds only after we introduce
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+groupoids"} smooth groupoids
            (below in
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#SmoothGroupoids"}> Smooth homotopy type - Model Layer - Smooth groupoids
                )
            %em still
            simpler than smooth manifolds, and of course even more expressive than smooth sets. Moreover, smooth groupoids are at the very heart of the geometry of physics: modern fundamental physics is all based on the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+symmetry"}> gauge principle
            ” and in
            %em
              %a{:href => "#GaugeTransformationsInElectromagnetism"} Model Layer – Gauge transformations in electromagnetism
            we explain how, mathematically, this is essentially nothing but the theory of smooth groupoids. As further background information we discuss in
            %em
              %a{:href => "#SmoothHomotopyTypesSemanticLayer"} Smooth homotopy types - Semantic Layer
            how this means that we are working in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} higher topos
            over abstract coordinate systems, and in
            %em
              %a{:href => "#SmoothHomotopyTypesSyntacticLayer"} Smooth homotopy type - Syntactic Layer
            how this means that we are reasoning about physics using the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            rules of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %p From this setup then naturally flow all the many structures and phenomena seen in the geometry of physics:
          %h3#Layers Layers of exposition
          %p
            We discuss each topic below in three stages, in three
            = succeed "." do
              %em layers
          %ol#LayerMod
            %li
              The first layer, called the
              = succeed "," do
                %strong Model Layer
              %em Model Layer
              does invoke the basic notion of a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              and of a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              – which are as simple as the notions of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
              or
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} algebra
              –, it does not use any actual
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %ol#LayerSem
            %li
              The second layer, called the
              = succeed "," do
                %strong Semantic Layer
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category+theory"} higher
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
              and (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos+theory"}> higher
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
              at work in the background. This puts the concrete constructions of the
              %em Model Layer
              into a more general context and helps to see certain organizational patterns that underlie the seemingly different phenomena. It provides some powerful theorems which the
              %em Model Layer
              is secretly benefitting from. For instance this layer gives a systematic rule for generalizing everything at the beginning Model Layers from ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
              to what is called
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermionic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
              are formalized: the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
              constituents of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
          %ol#LayerSyn
            %li
              The third layer, called the
              = succeed "," do
                %strong Syntactic Layer
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+framework"} formal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
              – which is
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
              – and of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} higher topos
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} smooth higher groupoids
              – which is
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos+theory"} higher
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
              used in
              = succeed "," do
                %em Semantic Layer
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} natural construction principle
              for objects in a (higher) topos that model some intended
              %em meaning
              – which is precisely what
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
              is all about. This is meant for readers who enjoy seeing fundamental physics
              %em naturally
              rooted in genuinely fundamental mathematics, in
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
              from
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/practical+foundations"} practical foundations
          %p
            %strong The three layers
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong Model Layer
                –
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+particular"} concrete particular
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            %li
              %p
                %strong Semantic Layer
                –
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+general"} concrete general
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos+theory"} higher
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            %li
              %p
                %strong Syntactic Layer
                –
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+general"} abstract general
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
                in
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
          %p
            This
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos-theoretic
            perspective on fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            which is discussed here is mostly original in the identifications it makes (
            %a{:href => "#SchreiberCohesiveInfinityTopos"}> Schreiber
            ), but it draws insights and inspiration from (and maybe realizes) a vision already expressed since the 1960s by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
          %blockquote
            %p
              My own motivation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              for developing
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              came from my earlier study of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+physics"} continuum physics
              of general materials,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              involves powerful and clear physical ideas which unfortunately have been submerged under a mathematical apparatus including not only
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+sequences"} Cauchy sequences
              and countably additive
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measures"} measures
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charts"} charts
              for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
              and of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+limits"} inverse limits
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sobolev+space"} Sobolev
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nuclear+spaces"} nuclear spaces
              of intensively and extensively variable quantities. But, as Fichera lamented, all this apparatus may well be helpful in the solution of certain problems, but can the problems themselves and the needed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
              be stated in a direct and clear manner? And might this not lead to a simpler, equally rigorous account? (
              %a{:href => "#Lawvere2000"}> Lawvere, 2000
              )
          %p
            More historical pointers along these lines and further related material can also be found at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory+and+physics"} higher category theory and physics
          %p
            To give a survey of how the exposition below proceeds in the fashion of these three layers, the following section
            %em
              %a{:href => "#TabulatedIndex"} The full story in a few formal words
            provides what may be read as commented
            %em index
            to the central themes of the following text. Whereas the exposition below is organized to start each topic with the discussion of its concrete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#LayerMod"} Model layer
            %a{:href => "#LayerSem"} Semantic Layer
            and then finally to the abstract formal syntax in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#LayerMod"} Syntactic Layer
          %p
            The leftmost columns of the following tables formulate concepts in terms of ordinary language. The second columns translate that ordinary language fairly directly to the formal language of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"}> homotopy
            )
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            in a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"}> higher
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            by the rules of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth ∞-groupoids
          %p These tables show that fairly evident and naïve sounding statements in ordinary language turn under this translation into what is generally regarded as fairly sophisticated constructions. In fact some of these constructions have only been found by translating along the categorical semantics dictionary this way. So the following tables also serves to show how the general abstract discussion here is a means to facilitate reasoning about seemingly complicated concepts underlying fundamental physics:
          %h3#TabulatedIndex The full story in a few formal words
          %p
            We give an overview in the spirit of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#SyntheticQuantumFieldTheory"} Synthetic Quantum Field Theory
          %p
            The fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observed world
            is governed by what is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+theory"} quantum theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            and induced from this all fundamental physics ever tested in laboratories; but by all that is known also the remaining ingredient of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            is fundamentally a quantum theory, see at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            for comments).
          %p
            Two major axiomatizations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+theory"} quantum theory
            are known, namely
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
                where one axiomatizes the assignment of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+states"} spaces of states
                to pieces of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
                (the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"}> Schrödinger picture
                ” of quantum theory)
              %p fragments of which involve:
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+quantum+mechanics+in+terms+of+dagger-compact+categories"} finite quantum mechanics in terms of dagger-compact categories
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triples"} spectral triples
                    and
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+field+theory"} graph field theory
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-spectral+triples"} 2-spectral triples
                    and
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-models"} sigma-models
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reshetikhin-Turaev+construction"} Reshetikhin-Turaev construction
                    for 3d
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FFRS-formalism"} FRS-construction
                    of 2d
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
                    from this via
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holography"} holography
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+topological+quantum+field+theories"} extended topological quantum field theories
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
                where one axiomatizes the assignment of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
                to pieces of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
                (the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+picture"}> Heisenberg picture
                ” of quantum theory)
              %p fragments of which involve:
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+axioms"}> Wightman axioms
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+toposes"}> Bohr toposes
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/bachelor+thesis+Nuiten"} Bohrification of local nets of observables
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebras"}> factorization algebras
                    ,
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+homology"} factorization homology
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+chiral+homology"} topological chiral homology
          %p
            (For an attempt at a survey of the state of the art as of 2011 see the collection (
            %a{:href => "#SatiSchreiber"}> Sati-Schreiber
            )).
          %p
            But all fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
            observed in (or conjectured to underlie) nature arise by a process called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            from structures in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            (or are induced via a mechanism called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
            from such that do).
          %p This differential geometric data involves
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonlinear+functional"} functionals
                – called
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functionals"} action functionals
            %li
              %p
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} smooth "spaces"
                – called
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stacks"} moduli stacks
            %li
              %p
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometric
                structures such as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles"} fiber bundles
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connections
                – called
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force fields
            %li
              %p
                as well as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+bundles"} associated bundles
                – called
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter+fields"} matter fields
          %p
            Similar differential geometric structures are involved in the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of such an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            to an actual
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %p Hence there is a sequence:
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
                %th
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo →
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
                %th
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo →
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %tbody
          %p
            We discuss a formalization of central aspects of this entire sequence. Our development proceeds – as befits a theory of physics and hence of nature – via
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            from
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/practical+foundation+of+mathematics"} practical foundations
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            Fundamentally, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metalanguage"} language
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            is to be a language about
            = succeed ";" do
              %em existence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgements"} judgements
            of the form:
          %blockquote
            %p
              There is a thing
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              of type
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p For instance:
          %blockquote
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi U
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo )
                        %mo ×
                        %mi SU
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mn 2
                          %mo )
                        %mo ×
                        %mi SU
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mn 3
                          %mo )
                        %mo )
                      %mi conn
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X,\mathbf{B}\left(U\left(1\right)\times SU\left(2\right)\times SU\left(3\right)\right)_{conn}]
              of gauge fields on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            (Here the square bracket expression for a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of gauge fields will be incrementally explained in the following.)
          %p
            To be predictive, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metalanguage"} language
            for physics is moreover to be a language in which we can make
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deductions
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deductive+reasoning"} deduce
            further such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgements"} judgements
            from given ones. For instance:
          %blockquote
            %p
              Given a gauge field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
              as above, there is an underlying
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+instanton"} instanton
                  sector
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi UnderlyingBundle
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∇
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} UnderlyingBundle(\nabla)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi U
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo )
                        %mo ×
                        %mi SU
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mn 2
                          %mo )
                        %mo ×
                        %mi SU
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mn 3
                          %mo )
                        %mo )
                      %mo ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[X,\mathbf{B}\left(U\left(1\right)\times SU\left(2\right)\times SU\left(3\right)\right)\right]
              of instanton configurations in the standard model.
          %p
            Quantum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpositions"} superpositions
            of such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+instantons"} Yang-Mills instantons
            are the very substrate out of which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observed world
            is build: the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+liquid+in+quantum+chromodynamics"} instanton liquid in quantum chromodynamics
            %em
              %a{:href => "#Yang-MillsTheory"} Yang-Mills theory
            below.) We consider here a language to reason about such phenomena formally.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metalanguage"} formal language
            for such
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgements"} judgements
            about there being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            of some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
          %p
            %strong>
              Expressions in (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"}> dependent
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            \:
          %p (read columns 1+2 first, then 3+4)
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th ordinary language
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
                %th chapter
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+abstract"} general abstract
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+concrete"} general concrete
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+particular"} concrete particular
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} There is…
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mi …
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash \ldots
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  (We speak in the context of a (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"}> higher
                  )
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi H
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
                  = succeed "." do
                    %em place where things may be
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} higher
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
                  is sufficient.))
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  (A
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                  for
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                        %mo =
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} =
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} Sh
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmthMfd"} SmthMfd
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} )
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} higher
                  such topos:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                        %mo =
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} =
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smooth%E2%88%9EGrpd"} Smooth∞Grpd
                  or
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SynthDiff%E2%88%9EGrpd"} SynthDiff∞Grpd
                  or
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothSuper%E2%88%9EGrpd"} SmoothSuper∞Grpd
                  or …)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "#SmoothSpaces"} Smooth sets
                  and
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "#SmoothnGroupoids"} Smooth homotopy types
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  There is a thing
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                  of type
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash\; x \colon X
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  An
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/element"} element
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mo *
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi x
                          %mi X
                          %mo )
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Mor
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left(* \stackrel{x}{\to} X\right) \in Mor(\mathbf{H})
                  of an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi H
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  A point
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
