          %p
            But the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge principle
            reaches deeper:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
            themselves are subject to the gauge principle.
          %p
            In general it is meaningless to ask if two gauge transformations are equal, but we may ask if there is a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge transformation
            that transforms one gauge transformation into the other. In the physics literature such
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge-of-gauge+transformations"} gauge-of-gauge transformations
            are best known in their incarnation as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ghost-of-ghost+fields"} ghost-of-ghost fields
            in what is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} BRST complex
            of the given
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
          %p
            Careful analysis for instance of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization"} Dirac charge quantization
            of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
            shows that already quite mundane physical phenomena exhibit such
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+transformations"} higher gauge transformations
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
          %p
            In either case, our
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metalanguage"} formal language
            should not allow the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} deduction
            that gauge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences"} equivalences
            are themselves either
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equal
            or not, but only allow
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgements"} judgements
            of the following form:
          %blockquote
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+transformation"} gauge-of-gauge equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
