                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla_1, \nabla_2
          %p
            The flavor of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} propositional equality
            for which this is the case is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
          %p
            Since therefore a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in intensional type theory may contain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies"} homotopies
            between its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            of arbitrary order, we call it a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
          %p
            The homotopy-type nature of the type of gauge connections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi conn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X,\mathbf{B}G_{conn}]
            is most familiar in the physics literature in its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-Lie+theory"} infinitesimal approximation
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} BRST complex
            of the gauge theory: the
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ghost-of-ghost+fields"} ghost-of-ghost fields
            in the BRST complex correspond to the
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies"} homotopies
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi conn
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X, \mathbf{B}G_{conn}]
          %p
            In particular, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
            we find the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            of a homotopy type, as indicated in the following table.
